// =============================================================================
// ui/tsconfig.json
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Purpose:
//   TypeScript project for the BROWSER half of the ui tree: every source file
//   under ui/src. It type-checks that tree and never emits from it. Its sibling
//   ui/tsconfig.node.json covers the three Node-executed members it names --
//   vite.config.ts, vitest.config.ts and documentationGate.test.ts -- and the two
//   projects are disjoint by design.
//   Refactoring Rationale: this sentence described that sibling as covering "the
//   two Node-executed config files", which understated it by one and mislabelled
//   the third: documentationGate.test.ts is a test rather than a config file, and
//   it sits there because it shells out to ESLint and needs Node globals the
//   browser project withholds.
//
//   Current source coverage is whatever exists under ui/src, because `include`
//   names the directory rather than a file list: today that is the three card
//   screens, the app shell and layout band, the routing tables, the API client,
//   the theme bridge, the message catalog and the tests beside them. The migration
//   target is all 21 online screens (docs/architecture/service-catalog.md); naming
//   the directory is what lets each one join the checked set by existing.
//
//   Three consumers depend on this project. ui/package.json's `typecheck` script
//   runs `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json` and then the same command against
//   tsconfig.node.json, so BOTH projects are compiler-checked; that manifest's
//   `build` script delegates to `typecheck` and reaches `vite build` only if both
//   pass. ui/eslint.config.js runs no tsc but names this project as the source of
//   type information for typescript-eslint, which is what lets the
//   eslint-plugin-jsdoc rules enforcing Rule 1 evaluate rather than skip.
//   .github/workflows/ui-ci.yml invokes the same `typecheck` script as a required
//   step. All three rest on the `include` at the foot of this file.
//
// Assumptions:
//   The compiler is typescript 6.0.3 as pinned in ui/package.json, and it stays
//   there. typescript-eslint 8.65.0 peer-declares `typescript >=4.8.4 <6.1.0`;
//   raising the compiler past that ceiling stops the type-aware lint pass, and
//   with it the JSDoc documentation gate, while the build stays green -- so the
//   loss is invisible to whoever raised it. Every option below is available at
//   6.0.3.
// Alternatives Considered:
//   The stock Vite react-ts layout, whose root tsconfig.json pairs an empty
//   `files` array with a `references` list. Rejected: `tsc -p` does not traverse
//   project references and no consumer here invokes `tsc -b`, so a references-only
//   root would make all three invocations check ZERO files and still exit 0, and
//   would leave typescript-eslint with no typed source. Two gates reporting
//   success having verified nothing. This file is therefore the app project
//   itself and no tsconfig.app.json exists.
// =============================================================================
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    // Assumptions: ES2022 is the level this tree is validated against at both
    // ends -- the build stage runs node:22.23.2-alpine and ui/package.json
    // requires node `^22.23.2 || >=24.18.1`, and the bundle is served to modern
    // browsers through CloudFront with no legacy floor stated anywhere in the
    // migration plan. A lower target would down-level constructs every supported
    // runtime implements, enlarging the bundle for a browser this deployment does
    // not target.
    "target": "ES2022",

    // Assumptions: this half runs in a browser, so DOM is in scope here exactly
    // as it is withheld from the sibling project. "DOM.Iterable" is listed
    // separately because "DOM" does not imply it: without it, `for...of` over a
    // `NodeList` and `FormData.entries()` are type errors rather than the
    // ordinary iteration they are at run time.
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],

    // Assumptions: these two answer one question -- who resolves and loads a
    // module here -- and the answer is Vite, not Node. ui/package.json declares
    // `type: module`, so `module` names the newest ESM setting and keeps
    // `import.meta` and top-level `await` available. Resolution is `bundler`
    // because Vite reads package `exports` maps the way a bundler does.
    // Alternatives Considered: `node16` and `nodenext`, each applying Node's own
    // ESM rules. Rejected because both demand an explicit file extension on every
    // relative import, and this tree is authored with the plan's extensionless
    // relative specifiers such as `../../theme/tokens`.
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",

    // Assumptions: the React 17+ automatic JSX runtime, which is why no .tsx file
    // here imports React merely to use JSX. Alternatives Considered: "react", the
    // classic runtime, compiles JSX to `React.createElement` and would require a
    // meaningless `React` import in every screen file that noUnusedLocals could
    // not distinguish from a genuine one.
    "jsx": "react-jsx",

    // Trade-offs: the list is EMPTY and empty is not absent. An empty array
    // suppresses automatic inclusion of every installed @types package; deleting
    // the key would pull all of them in, @types/node among them, handing browser
    // code `process`, `__dirname`, `Buffer` and `require` even though those are
    // installed here only for the sibling project. A type system that accepts
    // `process.env.API_URL` inside a React component is worse than none on that
    // line, because it certifies a reference with no value in a browser. The
    // consequence is load-bearing: browser code reads configuration through
    // Vite's `import.meta.env`.
    // Assumptions: that `import.meta.env` typing does NOT come from this list. It
    // arrives through the triple-slash reference in ui/src/vite-env.d.ts, which
    // resolves even here, because a populated `types` array suppresses only
    // AUTOMATIC inclusion and never an explicit reference. Deleting that file
    // fails every screen reading the API base URL, as TS2339 on `import.meta`.
    // Alternatives Considered: naming vite/client in this list instead, the reflex
    // fix when that expression fails to compile. Rejected because this list
    // applies to every file at once and vite/client carries 65 ambient
    // `declare module` statements for CSS-module and asset specifiers alongside
    // the env typing, so the empty list would stop meaning anything. A single
    // declaration file keeps the widening local.
    // Trade-offs: test-only ambient declarations are withheld for the same
    // reason, and THIS empty list is what obliges a test to import `describe`,
    // `it` and `expect` from 'vitest' by name -- with nothing declared ambiently,
    // an omitted import fails to compile on the symbol it omitted. Refactoring
    // Rationale: this comment credited `globals: false` in ui/vitest.config.ts for
    // that obligation, and that option is set to `true` there, for the separate
    // reason its own note records. Naming an option that says the opposite left a
    // reader with two documents disagreeing and no way to tell which enforced the
    // rule. ui/src/test/setup.ts imports '@testing-library/jest-dom/vitest', whose
    // module augmentation reaches every test through an import rather than through
    // an ambient global that production code could also reach.
    "types": [],

    // Assumptions: Vite and esbuild own emission, so no consumer reads a tsc
    // output -- both invocations pass --noEmit and `vite build` writes the only
    // deployed bundle. Emitting as well would produce a second JavaScript tree
    // beside the sources that nothing imports and .gitignore does not name.
    "noEmit": true,

    // Trade-offs: full strictness is accepted and the cost is real -- narrowing,
    // explicit null handling and exhaustive returns make the screens more verbose.
    // It is taken because the screens are transcriptions of COBOL whose observable
    // behaviour has to match a golden-master baseline, and the class of defect
    // strictness catches presents as a blank field or a crashed render rather than
    // as an obvious error.
    "strict": true,

    // Assumptions: a page of browse results is a fixed-arity array the final page
    // only partly fills, so indexing it can genuinely yield nothing. The baseline
    // is explicit and the arity differs per screen -- 7 rows in
    // app/cbl/COCRDLIC.cbl:255, 10 in app/cbl/COUSR00C.cbl:57 -- and both programs
    // guard every slot individually. Without this flag tsc types `rows[6]` as
    // present on a page holding four, and the screen reads a property of undefined
    // at render time instead of handling the short page as the COBOL does.
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,

    // Assumptions: the keyset envelope distinguishes "no further page" from "no
    // key supplied", and that is data. The baseline carries it in a dedicated
    // indicator (app/cbl/COCRDLIC.cbl:242-244) beside the first-key and last-key
    // pairs. Without this flag `firstKey?: string` also admits an explicit
    // `undefined`, so the two states collapse into one -- taking the enabled state
    // of the paging controls with them.
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,

    // Trade-offs: these four are not implied by `strict` and each rejects
    // something legal. In this tree every one of them is a half-finished edit
    // rather than an intentional construct: an unused local is usually a field
    // renamed on one side only, a fallthrough case a missing `break` in a PF-key
    // dispatch, a value-less return path an unhandled branch of a validation chain
    // transcribed from COBOL.
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,

    // Assumptions: a member meaning to replace an inherited one says so. Cheap in
    // a tree of function components; where a class does exist, an `override` that
    // silently stopped overriding after a base signature changed is the failure
    // this rejects.
    "noImplicitOverride": true,

    // Assumptions: esbuild, which transforms this tree under both Vite and
    // Vitest, compiles one file at a time and cannot look across a module
    // boundary to decide whether an imported name is a type or a value. These two
    // reject exactly the constructs it would mishandle, so a type-only import
    // must be written `import type` rather than erased by luck -- otherwise it can
    // survive into the bundle as a runtime import of a name that exports no value,
    // failing when the browser evaluates it and not when anything checks it.
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,

    // Trade-offs: dependency declaration files are not re-checked. The
    // alternative spends the check re-validating third-party .d.ts files this
    // project cannot edit, across antd, the React 19 typings and the Vitest chain,
    // where a conflict inside a transitive dependency would block a check whose
    // real subject is ui/src.
    "skipLibCheck": true,

    // Assumptions: parts of the dependency surface are still published as
    // CommonJS, so a default import of one has to be interoperable here for the
    // code to consume it the way Vite's bundling will.
    "esModuleInterop": true,

    // Assumptions: CI and the container build stage use a case-sensitive
    // filesystem, so a specifier whose casing differs from the file on disk has to
    // fail here rather than resolving on a case-insensitive developer machine and
    // breaking once the image is built.
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,

    // Assumptions: importing declarative data typed from its real shape beats
    // hand-declaring the same data twice, because the second copy is what drifts.
    "resolveJsonModule": true,

    // Assumptions: legal only alongside `noEmit`, and set for the narrow case of a
    // specifier naming a .ts or .tsx file explicitly. Vite resolves such a
    // specifier, and since nothing is emitted the extension cannot leak into an
    // output file a runtime would fail to resolve.
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,

    // Assumptions: class fields are defined rather than assigned, matching the
    // semantics `target: "ES2022"` already implies and esbuild applies. Stating it
    // keeps the two halves of the tree from disagreeing about field
    // initialisation order if either target moves.
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,

    // Assumptions: every file in ui/src is a module unconditionally, rather than
    // classified by whether it carries a top-level import or export. Left to that
    // inference, a file exporting nothing would be read as a global script and its
    // declarations would land in the global scope, where they could collide.
    "moduleDetection": "force"

    // Alternatives Considered: `baseUrl` with a `paths` alias -- the conventional
    // `@/theme/tokens` form. Rejected because the plan's import discipline
    // specifies relative specifiers and every module here is authored that way, so
    // an alias would create a second equally valid spelling with nothing to
    // arbitrate between them; Vite resolves the alias only if its own config
    // repeats the mapping, so the two would drift.
    //
    // Also deliberately unset: `incremental`, `tsBuildInfoFile` and `composite`,
    // because no consumer runs build mode and nothing references this project; and
    // `declaration`, `outDir`, `rootDir`, `allowJs` and `checkJs`, which describe
    // emission or JavaScript sources this project has neither of.
  },

  // Alternatives Considered: ui/src is included DIRECTLY and this project
  // declares no `references` and no empty `files` array. This is the single
  // load-bearing line in the file, for the reason given in the header: every
  // caller reaches this project through `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json`, and
  // under the stock references-only shape each would resolve an empty file list,
  // check nothing and exit 0 -- while ui/eslint.config.js inherited the same
  // emptiness and silently disabled the JSDoc gate. Two gates, one root cause.
  // Naming the directory rather than a glob is what keeps that fixed: `src`
  // matches every .ts and .tsx beneath it, ui/src/test included.
  "include": ["src"],

  // Assumptions: both entries are generated -- node_modules by `npm ci`, dist/ by
  // `vite build` -- and the root .gitignore names both. Checking generated output
  // reports errors nobody can fix in place, and a bundle re-checked as source is
  // the one place this strictness produces noise instead of signal. `src` already
  // excludes them; they are named so the boundary survives a later widening of the
  // include list.
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist"]
}
